/* book exam traps: A multiplier scales carrier and deviation together. A mixer shifts the carrier only; it can never change the deviation. | Check the narrowband condition at the lowest audio frequency, where β = Δf/fm is largest, not at 15 kHz. | Do not use one long multiplier and then complain the carrier overshoots: the mixer between the two multipliers is what lets the deviation reach 75 kHz while the carrier lands on the channel. | Varactor capacitance falls as reverse voltage rises, so the frequency rises with reverse voltage. Equal capacitance steps do not give equal frequency steps. | Centre-frequency drift and deviation are different. AFC corrects slow drift of the centre, not the audio deviation. */
