/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A parallel tuned circuit's voltage peaks at its resonant frequency fr and falls away on both flanks.
   - |V/Vpeak| = 1/√(1 + (2Q(f − fr)/fr)²)
   - Q is the quality factor of the tank; f is the instantaneous frequency and fr the resonant frequency, both in Hz; the formula holds close to resonance.
   - Two faults.
   - The balanced slope detector uses one primary tuned to fc and two secondary tanks, one tuned above fc and one below, each with its own diode.
   - One transformer: the primary is tuned to fc, and the secondary is also tuned to fc and centre-tapped.
   - A tuned secondary driven through mutual inductance is 90° out of phase with the primary at resonance.
   - Diode D1 sees the phasor sum ep + es/2 and diode D2 sees ep − es/2.
   - Near fc the secondary phase changes almost linearly with frequency, and near 90° the difference of the two lengths changes almost linearly with the phase; one nearly linear law applied after the other gives a wide straight region.
   - Turn one diode round and connect a large capacitor C across both loads.
*/
