/* book Worked example: Q: A slope detector for a 455 kHz narrowband FM signal with ±5 kHz deviation uses a tank tuned to fr = 465 kHz with Q = 50, driven at 1 V peak at resonance. Find the tank voltage at fc and at the two frequency extremes, and comment on linearity.
   1. Bandwidth fr/Q = 465/50 = 9.3 kHz; the carrier sits 10 kHz below fr, on the lower flank.
   2. At fc = 455 kHz: x = 2 × 50 × (455 − 465)/465 = −2.151; V = 1/√(1 + 2.151²) = 0.422 V.
   3. At 460 kHz: x = −1.075; V = 0.681 V, a rise of 0.259 V.
   4. At 450 kHz: x = −3.226; V = 0.296 V, a fall of 0.126 V.
   5. The positive swing is about twice the negative one, so the audio is badly distorted. The straight part of the flank is much narrower than ±5 kHz here; a lower Q or a balanced detector is needed.
   Answer: Tank voltage 0.422 V at fc, 0.681 V at 460 kHz and 0.296 V at 450 kHz: swings of +0.259 and −0.126 V, so a single slope detector distorts even ±5 kHz. */
