/* book Worked example 2: Q: A Foster-Seeley discriminator at 10.7 MHz has ep = 1 V and |es/2| = 1 V at fc, with secondary Q = 20. Find V1, V2 and vo at fc, at fc + 50 kHz and at fc − 50 kHz. What would a ratio detector give at +50 kHz?
   1. At fc: es/2 = j1 V, so V1 = V2 = |1 + j| = 1.414 V and vo = 0.
   2. At +50 kHz: x = 2 × 20 × 50 × 10³/(10.7 × 10⁶) = 0.1869. es/2 = j/(1 + jx) = 0.983 V at 79.4°, that is 0.181 + j0.966.
   3. V1 = |1.181 + j0.966| = 1.526 V; V2 = |0.819 − j0.966| = 1.267 V; vo = 0.259 V.
   4. At −50 kHz the angle is 100.6°, so V1 = 1.267 V and V2 = 1.526 V: vo = −0.259 V, the same size with the sign reversed.
   5. Ratio detector at +50 kHz: (V1 − V2)/2 = 0.129 V, half the Foster-Seeley output, while V1 + V2 = 2.79 V stays close to its 2.83 V value at fc.
   Answer: vo = 0 at fc and ±0.259 V at ±50 kHz (V1 = 1.526 V, V2 = 1.267 V); the ratio detector gives 0.129 V. */
